\title{Observer-Dependent Observable Geometry:\\Consensus, Channel Addition, and Collapse on the Real Line}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained topological model in which an observer channel is represented by a topology on a common state space and the geometry observable to a finite network is the intersection of its channel topologies. This consensus contains exactly the regions certified as open by every channel. We prove that after adjoining a topology $\mathcal T$ to a network with consensus $\mathcal C$, a set is open precisely when it belongs to both $\mathcal T$ and $\mathcal C$. Consequently, adding channels can only coarsen consensus, with strictness exactly when the new channel rejects a previously shared open set. On $\R$, a one-channel network using the lower-limit topology observes that topology itself, while two channels combining the lower- and upper-limit topologies observe exactly Euclidean topology. Hence complexities one and two yield strictly different observable geometries on the same carrier. The interval $[0,1)$ is a witness: it is open for the one-channel network and not for the two-channel network. We discuss finite algorithms, conservative multisensor fusion, limitations, and empirical extensions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Geometry is ordinarily assigned to a space independently of those who inspect it. Yet operational systems expose only selected subsets of a state space: a sensor has resolvable events, an experiment has robustly detectable conditions, and a readout mechanism has stable distinctions. If several channels must agree before a region is accepted, their common vocabulary can itself have geometric structure.

Topology supplies a minimal language for this situation. A topology determines neighborhoods, continuity, convergence, and boundaries. We interpret each channel topology as its family of resolvable regions. The network uses conservative aggregation: a region is observable exactly when every channel regards it as open. The result is the intersection of the channel topologies.

This rule distinguishes private information from common certification. Adding a channel adds a constraint, so it may remove shared open regions even if the total private information increases. Our principal example combines two asymmetric topologies on the real line. The lower-limit topology is generated by $[a,b)$ and the upper-limit topology by $(a,b]$. Each is strictly finer than Euclidean topology, but their intersection is exactly Euclidean topology.

The construction is a conditional mathematical model. It does not by itself assert an empirical law about spacetime or neural perception. Such an interpretation requires an independently justified map from observations to topologies.

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{topology} $\mathcal T$ on a set $X$ is a family of subsets containing $\varnothing$ and $X$, closed under arbitrary unions, and closed under finite intersections. Its members are called open sets.
\end{definition}

A topology $\mathcal T_1$ is finer than $\mathcal T_2$ when $\mathcal T_2\subseteq\mathcal T_1$, and strictly finer when the inclusion is proper. Thus finer topologies license more subsets as open.

\begin{definition}
A family $\mathcal B$ of subsets of $X$ is a \emph{basis} if every point belongs to a basis element and intersections of two basis elements can be locally refined by a third. The generated topology consists of arbitrary unions of members of $\mathcal B$.
\end{definition}

We use three topologies on $\R$.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item The Euclidean topology $\mathcal E$ has intervals $(a,b)$ as a basis.
\item The lower-limit topology $\mathcal L$ has intervals $[a,b)$ as a basis.
\item The upper-limit topology $\mathcal U$ has intervals $(a,b]$ as a basis.
\end{itemize}
Every Euclidean-open set is open in both one-sided topologies, so
\[
\mathcal E\subseteq\mathcal L,
\qquad
\mathcal E\subseteq\mathcal U.
\]
The inclusions are strict: $[0,1)\in\mathcal L\setminus\mathcal E$ and $(0,1]\in\mathcal U\setminus\mathcal E$.

\section{Networks and consensus}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{finite perceptual network of complexity $n$} on $X$ is a family $\mathbf T=(\mathcal T_i)_{i=0}^{n-1}$ of topologies on $X$. Its \emph{observable geometry}, or \emph{consensus topology}, is
\[
\Con(\mathbf T)=\bigcap_{i=0}^{n-1}\mathcal T_i.
\]
Thus $A\subseteq X$ is consensus-open if and only if $A\in\mathcal T_i$ for every channel $i$.
\end{definition}

Channel count is used only as a structural complexity measure; it does not measure intelligence or computational cost.

\begin{proposition}[Consensus is a topology]
For every family of topologies $\mathbf T$ on $X$, the family $\Con(\mathbf T)$ is a topology on $X$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every channel contains $\varnothing$ and $X$. A union of sets open in every channel remains open in every channel, as does a finite intersection. Hence the intersection satisfies all topology axioms.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[One-channel identity]
For a one-channel network $\mathbf T=(\mathcal T)$,
\[
\Con(\mathbf T)=\mathcal T.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Membership in an intersection over a singleton family is membership in its sole member.
\end{proof}

In particular, if $\mathbf L=(\mathcal L)$, then $\Con(\mathbf L)=\mathcal L$.

\section{Adding a channel}

\begin{theorem}[Channel-addition criterion]
Let $\mathbf T=(\mathcal T_i)_{i\in I}$ be an observer family on $X$ with consensus $\mathcal C=\bigcap_{i\in I}\mathcal T_i$, and adjoin a channel with topology $\mathcal S$. For every $A\subseteq X$,
\[
A\in\Con(\mathcal S,\mathbf T)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
A\in\mathcal S\ \text{and}\ A\in\mathcal C.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\Con(\mathcal S,\mathbf T)=\mathcal S\cap\mathcal C.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $A$ belongs to the enlarged consensus, it is open in the new channel and every old channel. These statements are respectively $A\in\mathcal S$ and $A\in\mathcal C$. Conversely, those two conditions say exactly that $A$ is open in every channel of the enlarged family.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monotonicity]
Adding a channel can only coarsen consensus:
\[
\Con(\mathcal S,\mathbf T)\subseteq\Con(\mathbf T).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The enlarged consensus is $\mathcal S\cap\mathcal C$, which is contained in $\mathcal C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strictness criterion]
The preceding inclusion is strict if and only if some set $A$ satisfies
\[
A\in\Con(\mathbf T)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
A\notin\mathcal S.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The new consensus removes exactly those members of $\mathcal C$ that do not belong to $\mathcal S$. Thus a removed witness exists precisely when the inclusion is proper.
\end{proof}

\section{Collapse on the real line}
Let $\mathbf B=(\mathcal L,\mathcal U)$ be the two-channel network formed from opposite one-sided topologies.

\begin{lemma}[Euclidean opens survive]
Every Euclidean-open subset of $\R$ is open in both $\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal U$. Hence
\[
\mathcal E\subseteq\mathcal L\cap\mathcal U.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $V$ is Euclidean open and $x\in V$, choose $\varepsilon>0$ with $(x-\varepsilon,x+\varepsilon)\subseteq V$. Then $[x,x+\varepsilon)\subseteq V$ is a lower-limit neighborhood and $(x-\varepsilon,x]\subseteq V$ is an upper-limit neighborhood.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Opposite certificates form a two-sided neighborhood]
If $V$ is open in both $\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal U$, every $x\in V$ has $a<x<b$ such that
\[
(a,b)\subseteq V.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Lower-limit openness supplies $[x,b)\subseteq V$ for some $b>x$, while upper-limit openness supplies $(a,x]\subseteq V$ for some $a<x$. Their union is $(a,b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-channel Euclidean collapse]
The observable geometry of the lower/upper network is exactly Euclidean:
\[
\Con(\mathbf B)=\mathcal L\cap\mathcal U=\mathcal E.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first lemma gives $\mathcal E\subseteq\mathcal L\cap\mathcal U$. For the reverse inclusion, the second lemma gives an ordinary open neighborhood around each point of any set in $\mathcal L\cap\mathcal U$, which is the local criterion for Euclidean openness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict observer dependence]
The one-channel observable geometry is strictly finer than the two-channel observable geometry:
\[
\Con(\mathbf B)=\mathcal E
\subsetneq
\mathcal L=\Con(\mathbf L).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The one-channel identity and the collapse theorem identify the two consensus topologies. Euclidean topology is contained in lower-limit topology, and the inclusion is proper because $[0,1)$ is lower-limit open but not Euclidean open.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Complexity changes observable geometry]
There are networks of complexities one and two on the same carrier $\R$ whose observable geometries are unequal.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use $\mathbf L$ and $\mathbf B$, whose consensus topologies are respectively $\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal E$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Half-open state collapse]
The region $[0,1)$ is open for the one-channel network $\mathbf L$ and not open for the two-channel network $\mathbf B$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
It is a lower-limit basis element. It is not Euclidean open because every Euclidean neighborhood of $0$ contains negative points.
\end{proof}

The carrier never changes: only the family of subsets designated open changes. ``Collapse'' therefore means loss of observable status, not deletion of points.

\section{Finite algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Consensus intersection]
Given a finite carrier $X$ and channel topologies $\mathcal T_1,\ldots,\mathcal T_n$, each represented as a hash set of immutable subsets, initialize $\mathcal C=\mathcal T_1$ and successively replace $\mathcal C$ by $\mathcal C\cap\mathcal T_i$. Return $\mathcal C$.
\end{algorithm}

If every topology has at most $M$ open sets, expected running time is $O(nM)$ membership operations, excluding subset encoding. Since a topology on a $q$-point set can have $2^q$ opens, explicit representation is exponential in $q$ in the worst case.

\begin{algorithm}[Strict-collapse witness search]
Given an old consensus $\mathcal C$ and new topology $\mathcal S$, scan $A\in\mathcal C$. Return the first $A\notin\mathcal S$; if none exists, report that consensus is unchanged.
\end{algorithm}

With hash membership, this takes expected time $O(|\mathcal C|)$. Its correctness is the strictness criterion.

A grid demonstration on the real line can display directional interval predicates and show that $[0,1)$ is accepted by the lower-facing rule but fails a symmetric neighborhood test at $0$. Such a grid is illustrative rather than a proof of the infinite-space identity, because clipping and endpoint effects can alter topology. Exact finite experiments should specify genuine finite topologies and intersect their open-set families.

\section{Applications}

\subsection{Conservative multisensor fusion}
If a controller acts only on regions certified by every sensor, its operational event structure is a consensus topology. A new sensor cannot create unanimously certified events. A lost open set is an explicit disagreement witness, making strict changes auditable.

\subsection{Neural-state readouts}
For a finite neural transition graph, one may assign each readout the topology generated by subsets it can reliably distinguish. Consensus then records distinctions stable across every readout. This requires empirical definitions of distinguishability, noise tolerance, and fusion policy. Once supplied, the strictness criterion is testable by finite enumeration.

\subsection{Scientific observability}
Different experimental modalities may support different robust event families. Their consensus captures conditions invariant across protocols, rather than the union of everything detected somewhere. A union of topologies need not itself be a topology, while their intersection always is.

\subsection{Continuity}
Changing consensus changes continuity. Coarsening the topology on a codomain generally makes continuity into it easier; coarsening the topology on a domain generally makes continuity out of it harder. Observer architecture can therefore affect which transformations count as observationally smooth.

\section{Limitations}
The unanimity rule is a modeling choice. Majority, weighted, and confidence-threshold fusion need not produce a topology without an additional closure operation. Channel count is also a coarse complexity measure: duplicate channels change nothing, while one channel may resolve many more opens than several others.

Topology omits metric, probabilistic, and temporal information. It records qualitative stability, not distance, likelihood, latency, or noise. The real-line channels are ideal directional resolutions, whereas physical sensors have finite precision. Empirical use may require finite generated topologies, measures, filtrations, or probabilistic neighborhoods.

Most importantly, the theorem does not imply that physical spacetime changes when observed. It says that a topology defined as unanimous observability depends on the chosen channel family. An ontological claim requires premises not contained in this model.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{Finite observer-complexity monotonicity.} Develop a uniform indexed theorem for extensions from $n$ to $n+1$ channels.
\item \textbf{Strictness under extension.} Extract computable witnesses for consensus loss on finite spaces.
\item \textbf{Minimal observer complexity.} For a finite $X$ and target topology $\mathcal T$, find the least $n$ for which $\mathcal T$ is the consensus of $n$ strict refinements, and bound it using $|\mathcal T|$.
\item \textbf{Product compositionality.} Characterize when consensus of channelwise products equals the product of the two consensus topologies.
\item \textbf{Empirical encoding.} Generate readout topologies from measured distinguishability data and test channel-addition predictions.
\item \textbf{Approximate consensus.} Study threshold agreement, whose accepted family need not itself be topological.
\item \textbf{Dynamic channels.} Analyze abrupt changes in consensus as channel topologies vary over time.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
Euclidean geometry emerges here as common ground between two directionally biased resolutions. This is not metric averaging or probabilistic mixing; it is logical conjunction of openness. All Euclidean opens survive both channels. A set with only a right-facing certificate can fail the upper-limit test, and vice versa. What survives both has two one-sided certificates at every point, which combine into an ordinary interval.

The witness $[0,1)$ localizes the phenomenon at its left endpoint. Lower-limit geometry permits a neighborhood beginning exactly at $0$ and extending rightward. Euclidean geometry demands room on both sides. The added upper-limit channel imposes that missing demand, removing the half-open region from common certification.

Consensus is thus antitone in constraints: adding tests may increase private evidence while shrinking the feasible family that passes every test. Topology makes this familiar logical fact geometrically consequential.

\section{Conclusion}
A finite network of topological channels has a natural conservative observable geometry: the intersection of its channel topologies. Adding one channel intersects the old consensus with one more topology, so it can only preserve or remove open regions. Strict change occurs exactly when the new channel vetoes an old consensus-open set.

On $\R$, one lower-limit channel observes lower-limit geometry, whereas adding its upper-limit counterpart yields precisely Euclidean geometry. The inclusion is strict, witnessed by $[0,1)$. The same carrier therefore supports networks of complexities one and two with different observable geometries. The model does not turn observation into metaphysics; it turns agreement into topology, providing a precise mechanism suitable for finite computation and, given an empirical encoding, falsifiable application.

